      "id": "8ba8165c-e904-4534-9de9-0d2f465c674f",
      "name": "Is Evaluation"
    },
    {
      "parameters": {
        "operation": "setMetrics",
        "expectedAnswer": "={{ $('Eval').item.json.expected_root_cause }}",
        "actualAnswer": "={{ $('Reporter').item.json.output.root_cause }}",
        "prompt": "=# Root Cause Evaluation\n\nYou are an expert evaluator that scores how well an AI agent's identified root cause matches the actual (expected) root cause. You will be given two inputs:\n\n1. **Expected Root Cause** — The ground-truth root cause determined by a human expert.\n2. **Actual Root Cause** — The root cause returned by the AI agent being evaluated.\n\n## Scoring Rubric (1–5 Scale)\n### Score 5 — Exact or Near-Perfect Match\n- The actual root cause identifies the **same underlying issue** as the expected root cause.\n- Minor differences in wording, phrasing, or additional (but non-contradictory) detail are acceptable.\n\n### Score 4 — Substantially Correct with Minor Gaps\n- The actual root cause identifies the **correct general area** and gets the primary cause right, but misses a secondary detail, or includes a minor inaccuracy that doesn't change the overall diagnosis.\n- Minor differences in wording, phrasing, or additional (but non-contradictory) detail are acceptable.\n\n### Score 3 — Partially Correct\n- The actual root cause overlaps with the expected root cause but is **incomplete or diluted** with other incorrect or irrelevant causes.\n- It may identify a symptom rather than the true root cause, or identify the right subsystem but the wrong failure mode.\n\n### Score 2 — Mostly Incorrect\n- The actual root cause is **largely wrong** but contains a minor tangential connection to the real issue (e.g., mentions the right service but identifies a completely wrong failure).\n- Or the AI fabricates a plausible-sounding root cause that is not supported by the evidence.\n\n### Score 1 — Completely Incorrect\n- The actual root cause is **entirely wrong**, unrelated, or contradicts the expected root cause.\n- Also applies when the expected root cause is \"nothing was found\" / \"no root cause identifiable\" but the AI invents a root cause anyway — this is a **hallucination** and should be penalized heavily.\n\n## Special Cases\n- **Expected = \"Nothing was found\" / \"No root cause identifiable\"**: If the AI also concludes that no root cause can be determined, score **5**. If the AI fabricates or guesses a root cause, score **1** or **2** depending on how speculative vs. confidently wrong it is.\n- **Actual = \"Nothing was found\" / \"No root cause identifiable\"**: If the expected root cause is a real identifiable issue but the AI fails to find it, score **1** or **2**.\n- **Multiple root causes**: If the expected root cause lists multiple factors, evaluate whether the actual root cause captures all, some, or none of them, and score proportionally.\n\n## Evaluation Rules\n1. **Focus on semantic equivalence**, not lexical similarity. Different words describing the same root cause should score high.\n2. **Ignore extra context** in the actual root cause that is accurate and non-contradictory — it should not lower the score.\n3. **Penalize hallucinations** — confidently stated root causes that are fabricated or unsupported should score low.\n4. **Penalize vagueness** — if the actual root cause is so generic it could apply to anything (e.g., \"there is a configuration issue\"), score lower unless the expected root cause is equally vague.\n5. **Order of importance**: Correctness of the core root cause > completeness of details > wording/phrasing.",
        "options": {
          "metricName": "Correctness",
          "inputPrompt": "Actual Root Cause: {actual_answer}\n\nExpected Root Cause: {expected_answer}"
        }
      },
